answer:
To allow and facilitate cooperation with the public and private sectors, AI regulatory sandboxes facilitate the involvement of relevant actors within the AI ecosystem, specifically [Art. 58(2)(f)]:

* Notified bodies [Art. 58(2)(f)];
* Standardisation organisations [Art. 58(2)(f)];
* SMEs, including start-ups [Art. 58(2)(f)];
* Enterprises and innovators [Art. 58(2)(f)];
* Testing and experimentation facilities [Art. 58(2)(f)];
* Research and experimentation labs [Art. 58(2)(f)];
* European Digital Innovation Hubs [Art. 58(2)(f)];
* Centres of excellence [Art. 58(2)(f)];
* Individual researchers [Art. 58(2)(f)].

In addition, other parties and public authorities may be involved or cooperate in the sandboxes:
* **Deployers and other relevant third parties**, who may partner with providers and prospective providers to submit applications [Art. 58(2)(b)].
* **Other relevant authorities**, with whom national competent authorities cooperate where appropriate [Art. 57(4)], including authorities supervising other regulatory sandboxes established under Union or national law [Art. 57(4)].
* **National data protection authorities and other competent authorities** providing or supporting access to data (or under whose supervisory remit the AI systems fall), who are associated with the operation of the sandbox and its supervision [Art. 57(10)].
* **The AI Office and the Board**, who may be asked by national competent authorities for support and guidance [Art. 57(15)].

Based on the provided excerpts, the large-scale EU IT systems and databases in the area of Freedom, Security, and Justice listed in Annex X are:

* **Eurodac** (for the comparison of biometric data to identify illegally staying third-country nationals and stateless persons, apply asylum regulations, and for law enforcement access) [Annex X, points 3–5];
* **Entry/Exit System (EES)** (to register entry, exit, and refusal of entry data of third-country nationals crossing external borders) [Annex X, points 3–5];
* **European Travel Information and Authorisation System (ETIAS)** [Annex X, points 3–5];
* **European Criminal Records Information System on third-country nationals and stateless persons (ECRIS-TCN)** [Annex X, points 6–7];
* **Interoperability** frameworks establishing interoperability between EU information systems in the field of borders and visas, as well as police and judicial cooperation, asylum, and migration [Annex X, points 6–7].

Additionally, the Act establishes an **EU database for high-risk AI systems**, maintained by the Commission, which includes a dedicated "secure non-public section" for registering high-risk AI systems used in migration, asylum, and border control management [Art. 49(4)], [Art. 71(1)].

Under Annex VII, point 3.1, the application of the provider must include the following information and documentation [Annex VII, point 3]:

* The name and address of the provider and, if lodged by an authorised representative, their name and address as well [Annex VII, point 3];
* The list of AI systems covered under the same quality management system [Annex VII, point 3];
* The technical documentation for each AI system covered under that quality management system [Annex VII, point 3];
* Documentation concerning the quality management system covering all aspects listed under Article 17 [Annex VII, point 3];
* A description of the procedures in place to ensure that the quality management system remains adequate and effective [Annex VII, point 3];
* A written declaration that the same application has not been lodged with any other notified body [Annex VII, point 3].

Regarding the documentation covering Article 17, the provided excerpts specify that the quality management system must include:
* A strategy for regulatory compliance, including compliance with conformity assessment procedures and management of modifications [Art. 17(1)(a)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for design, design control, and design verification [Art. 17(1)(b)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for development, quality control, and quality assurance [Art. 17(1)(c)];
* Examination, test, and validation procedures carried out before, during, and after development, along with their frequency [Art. 17(1)(d)];
* Technical specifications and standards to be applied, including means used to ensure compliance where harmonised standards are not fully applied or do not cover all relevant requirements [Art. 17(1)(e)];
* The risk management system referred to in Article 9 [Art. 17(1)(g)];
* The setting-up, implementation, and maintenance of a post-market monitoring system in accordance with Article 72 [Art. 17(1)(h)].

If an AI provider's arguments are not sufficiently substantiated and it fails to demonstrate that its general-purpose AI model does not present systemic risks, the Commission rejects those arguments, and the model is considered to be a general-purpose AI model with systemic risk [Art. 52(3)]. 

Consequently, the provider becomes subject to specific additional obligations (in addition to those in Articles 53 and 54), requiring it to [Art. 55(1)]:

* Perform model evaluations using standardised protocols and tools reflecting the state of the art, including conducting and documenting adversarial testing to identify and mitigate systemic risks [Art. 55(1)(a)];
* Assess and mitigate possible systemic risks at Union level stemming from the model's development, placing on the market, or use [Art. 55(1)(b)];
* Track, document, and report relevant information on serious incidents and possible corrective measures without undue delay to the AI Office and, where appropriate, national competent authorities [Art. 55(1)(c)];
* Ensure an adequate level of cybersecurity protection for the model and its physical infrastructure [Art. 55(1)(d)].

For a Union institution, body, office, or agency, non-compliance with the prohibition of the AI practices referred to in Article 5 is subject to administrative fines of up to EUR 1 500 000 [Art. 100(2)].

Key conditions and factors:
* Administrative fines on Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies are imposed by the European Data Protection Supervisor [Art. 100(1)].
* When deciding whether to impose a fine and determining its amount, all relevant circumstances must be taken into account, including:
  * the nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences [Art. 100(1)(a)];
  * the degree of responsibility and technical/organisational measures implemented [Art. 100(1)(b)];
  * actions taken to mitigate damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
  * the degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor [Art. 100(1)(d)];
  * any similar previous infringements [Art. 100(1)(e)];
  * how the infringement became known to the Supervisor [Art. 100(1)(f)];
  * the annual budget of the Union institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

Under Article 82(4) regarding compliant AI systems that present a risk, after national measures are taken, the Commission:

* "shall without undue delay enter into consultation with the Member States concerned and the relevant operators" [Art. 82(4)];
* "shall evaluate the national measures taken" [Art. 82(4)];
* "shall decide whether the measure is justified and, where necessary, propose other appropriate measures" on the basis of the results of that evaluation [Art. 82(4)].

*(Note: While other provisions require national authorities to inform or notify the Commission of their evaluations and provisional measures [Art. 79(3)], [Art. 79(5)], [Art. 80(3)], [Art. 82(3)], the provided excerpts do not describe further Commission actions for those specific procedures.)*

Under Article 92(4) of the EU AI Act, a request for access must include the following details:

* The legal basis [Art. 92(4)];
* The purpose and reasons of the request [Art. 92(4)];
* The period within which the access is to be provided [Art. 92(4)]; and
* The fines provided for in Article 101 for failure to provide access [Art. 92(4)].

Under the EU AI Act, if a notified body no longer meets the requirements, the notifying authority and the Commission must take action to investigate, restrict, suspend, or withdraw its designation, and address the impact on certificates:

* **Investigation and decision by the notifying authority**: If a notifying authority has sufficient reason to consider that a notified body no longer meets the requirements in Article 31 or fails to fulfil its obligations, it must "without delay investigate the matter with the utmost diligence" [Art. 36(4)]. It must inform the body of the objections raised and give it the opportunity to make its views known [Art. 36(4)]. If confirmed, the authority must "restrict, suspend or withdraw the designation as appropriate, depending on the seriousness of the failure", and immediately inform the Commission and other Member States [Art. 36(4)].
* **Impact on certificates**: If a designation is restricted, suspended, or withdrawn, the notifying authority must:
  * Assess the impact on certificates issued by the body [Art. 36(7)(a)];
  * Submit a report on its findings to the Commission and other Member States within three months of notifying the changes [Art. 36(7)(b)];
  * Require the body to suspend or withdraw any unduly issued certificates within a reasonable period to ensure continuing conformity of high-risk AI systems [Art. 36(7)(c)];
  * Inform the Commission and Member States about those certificates [Art. 36(7)(d)];
  * Provide relevant information to the national competent authorities where the provider is located, which must take appropriate measures if necessary to prevent risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights [Art. 36(7)(e)].
* **Action by the Commission**: If the Commission ascertains that a notified body does not meet or no longer meets requirements, it informs the notifying Member State and requests necessary corrective measures, including suspension or withdrawal [Art. 37(4)]. If the Member State fails to take those measures, the Commission may adopt an implementing act (in accordance with the examination procedure referred to in Article 98(2)) to "suspend, restrict or withdraw the designation" [Art. 37(4)].

When collecting and handling data about AI systems, authorities must adhere to the following rules under the provided excerpts:

* **Necessity and Minimisation:** Authorities must request only data that is strictly necessary to assess the risk posed by AI systems and to exercise their powers in accordance with Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 and Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 [Art. 78(2)].
* **Security and Deletion:** Authorities must implement adequate and effective cybersecurity measures to safeguard the security and confidentiality of information and data obtained [Art. 78(2)]. They must delete collected data as soon as it is no longer needed for the purpose for which it was obtained, in accordance with Union or national law [Art. 78(2)].
* **General Confidentiality Obligations:** Competent authorities, market surveillance authorities, the Commission, and notified bodies must treat obtained information and documentation in accordance with confidentiality obligations [Art. 21(3)], [Art. 74(14)], [Art. 78(1)]. They must protect:
  * Intellectual property rights, confidential business information, and trade secrets, including source code (except specific cases under Directive (EU) 2016/943) [Art. 78(1)(a)];
  * Effective implementation of the Regulation (notably for audits, inspections, or investigations) [Art. 78(1)(b)];
  * Public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
  * The conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
  * Classified information pursuant to Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].
* **Special Rules for Law Enforcement, Border Control, Immigration, or Asylum Contexts:**
  * Information exchanged confidentially between national competent authorities or with the Commission regarding high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1, 6, or 7 of Annex III used by these authorities must not be disclosed without prior consultation of the originating authority and the deployer if disclosure would jeopardise public and national security interests [Art. 78(3)].
  * Such information exchanges must not cover sensitive operational data related to the activities of these authorities [Art. 78(3)].
  * When these authorities are providers of those systems, the technical documentation must remain on their premises; they must grant market surveillance authorities immediate access or copies upon request, and only staff with the appropriate level of security clearance may access the documentation or copies [Art. 78(3)].

A general-purpose AI model is classified as a general-purpose AI model with systemic risk if it meets any of the following conditions:

* **High impact capabilities:** It has high impact capabilities evaluated on the basis of appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks [Art. 51(1)(a)]. A model is presumed to have high impact capabilities when the cumulative amount of computation used for its training measured in floating point operations is greater than $10^{25}$ [Art. 51(2)].
* **Commission decision of equivalent capabilities or impact:** Based on a decision of the Commission—acting *ex officio* or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel—it has capabilities or an impact equivalent to high impact capabilities, having regard to the criteria set out in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)(b)]. 

Additionally, under the classification procedures:
* A model is considered to be a model with systemic risk if the Commission concludes that arguments submitted by a provider are not sufficiently substantiated and the provider cannot demonstrate that the model does not present systemic risks due to its specific characteristics [Art. 52(3)].
* The Commission may decide to designate a model as presenting systemic risk if it becomes aware of one that has not been notified by its provider [Art. 52(1)].

**Criteria under Annex XIII:**
When determining whether a model has equivalent capabilities or impact, the Commission takes into account [Annex XIII]:
* The number of parameters [Annex XIII, point (a)];
* The quality or size of the data set (e.g. measured through tokens) [Annex XIII, point (b)];
* The amount of computation used for training (measured in floating point operations or indicated by variables like estimated cost, time, or energy consumption) [Annex XIII, point (c)];
* Input and output modalities (e.g., text to text, text to image, multi-modality, biological sequences) and state of the art thresholds for each modality [Annex XIII, point (d)];
* Benchmarks and capability evaluations (e.g., number of tasks without additional training, adaptability, autonomy, scalability, tool access) [Annex XIII, point (e)];
* Whether it has a high impact on the internal market due to reach, which is presumed if it has been made available to at least 10,000 registered business users established in the Union [Annex XIII, point (f)];
* The number of registered end-users [Annex XIII, point (g)].

*(Note: "Systemic risk" is defined as a risk specific to high-impact capabilities that has a significant impact on the Union market due to its reach, or due to actual or reasonably foreseeable negative effects on public health, safety, public security, fundamental rights, or society as a whole, that can propagate at scale across the value chain [Art. 3(65)].)*

When ensuring that a high-risk AI system complies with the requirements laid down in Section 2, the following must be taken into account:

* The system's "intended purpose" [Art. 8(1)];
* The "generally acknowledged state of the art on AI and AI-related technologies" [Art. 8(1)];
* The risk management system referred to in Article 9 [Art. 8(1)].

Yes. Under the EU AI Act, providers of high-risk AI systems are required to maintain records and keep documentation:

* **Record-keeping systems and procedures**: Providers must implement a quality management system that includes "systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information" [Art. 17(1)(k)].
* **10-year retention obligation**: Providers must keep specific documentation at the disposal of national competent authorities for a period ending 10 years after the high-risk AI system has been placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)]:
  * The technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * The documentation concerning the quality management system referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * The EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)], which must be drawn up in written machine-readable, physical, or electronically signed form, kept at the disposal of authorities for 10 years, and submitted upon request [Art. 47(1)].

The **European Data Protection Supervisor** may impose administrative fines on Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies falling within the scope of the Regulation [Art. 100(1)].

When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and determining its amount, all relevant circumstances of the specific situation must be taken into account, giving due regard to the following factors [Art. 100(1)]:

* the nature, gravity and duration of the infringement and of its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system concerned, and, where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered by them [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* the degree of responsibility of the Union institution, body, office or agency, taking into account technical and organisational measures implemented by them [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* any action taken to mitigate the damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* the degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor to remedy the infringement and mitigate its possible adverse effects, including compliance with any previously ordered measures regarding the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* any similar previous infringements by the institution, body, office or agency [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* the manner in which the infringement became known to the European Data Protection Supervisor, in particular whether and to what extent it was notified [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* the annual budget of the Union institution, body, office or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

Yes, if you are a provider of a high-risk AI system, you are required to establish, implement, document, and maintain a risk management system [Art. 9(1)], [Art. 16(a)]. 

Under the Act, this process entails the following:
* **Lifecycle process:** It must be a continuous iterative process planned and run throughout the entire lifecycle of the high-risk AI system, requiring regular systematic review and updating [Art. 9(2)].
* **Core steps:** The process must comprise:
  * identifying and analysing known and reasonably foreseeable risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights when used according to its intended purpose [Art. 9(2)(a)];
  * estimating and evaluating risks arising from use according to intended purpose and under conditions of reasonably foreseeable misuse [Art. 9(2)(b)];
  * evaluating other risks based on data gathered from the post-market monitoring system [Art. 9(2)(c)];
  * adopting appropriate and targeted risk management measures [Art. 9(2)(d)].
* **Integration into quality management:** Providers must also include the risk management system as an aspect of their mandatory quality management system [Art. 16(c)], [Art. 17(1)(g)].

This is not legal advice.

Under Article 53(1)(a), providers of general-purpose AI models must draw up and keep up-to-date technical documentation containing, at a minimum, the information set out in Annex XI to provide upon request to the AI Office and national competent authorities [Art. 53(1)(a)]. 

For all general-purpose AI models, this documentation must contain the following information as appropriate to the size and risk profile of the model [Annex XI, Section 1]:

* **General description of the model**, including [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1]:
  * The tasks it is intended to perform and the type and nature of AI systems into which it can be integrated [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(a)];
  * Applicable acceptable use policies [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(b)];
  * Date of release and distribution methods [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(c)];
  * Architecture and number of parameters [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(d)];
  * Modality (e.g., text, image) and format of inputs and outputs [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(e)];
  * The licence [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(f)].

* **Detailed description and development process information**, including [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2]:
  * Technical means (e.g., instructions of use, infrastructure, tools) required to integrate the model into AI systems [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(a)];
  * Design specifications of the model and training process, including methodologies, techniques, key design choices, rationale, assumptions, what the model optimizes for, and relevance of different parameters [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(b)];
  * Information on data used for training, testing, and validation (type, provenance, curation methodologies, number of data points, scope, acquisition/selection, and measures to detect unsuitable data sources and identifiable biases) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(c)];
  * Computational resources used for training (e.g., number of floating point operations), training time, and other training details [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(d)];
  * Known or estimated energy consumption (which may be estimated from computational resources if unknown) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(e)].

Additionally, providers of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk must include [Annex XI, Section 2, points 1–3]:
* A detailed description of evaluation strategies (including criteria, metrics, methodology for identifying limitations) and evaluation results [Annex XI, Section 2, points 1–3];
* Where applicable, a detailed description of measures for internal/external adversarial testing (e.g., red teaming) and model adaptations (such as alignment and fine-tuning) [Annex XI, Section 2, points 1–3];
* Where applicable, a detailed description of the system architecture showing how software components integrate [Annex XI, Section 2, points 1–3].

Yes. Under the EU AI Act, notified bodies are required to share assessment results with other notified bodies carrying out similar activities covering the same types of AI systems [Art. 45(3)].

Specifically, each notified body must:
* Provide those other notified bodies with "relevant information on issues relating to negative and, on request, positive conformity assessment results" [Art. 45(3)];
* Inform other notified bodies of quality management system approvals that it has "refused, suspended or withdrawn, and, upon request, of quality system approvals which it has issued" [Art. 45(2)(a)];
* Inform other notified bodies of Union technical documentation assessment certificates (or supplements) that it has "refused, withdrawn, suspended or otherwise restricted, and, upon request, of the certificates and/or supplements thereto which it has issued" [Art. 45(2)(b)].

If you are a deployer subject to the registration obligation under Article 49(3), the information you must provide and keep up to date in the EU database comprises [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5]:

* The name, address, and contact details of the deployer [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* The name, address, and contact details of the person submitting information on behalf of the deployer [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5] (specifically, natural persons responsible for registering who have legal authority to represent the deployer [Art. 71(5)]);
* The URL of the entry of the AI system in the EU database by its provider [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* A summary of the findings of the fundamental rights impact assessment conducted in accordance with Article 27 [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* Where applicable, a summary of the data protection impact assessment carried out in accordance with Article 35 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 or Article 27 of Directive (EU) 2016/680 as specified in Article 26(8) of the AI Act [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5].

Under the Act's criteria, this registration obligation applies before putting into service or using an Annex III high-risk AI system (except those listed in point 2 of Annex III) if you are a public authority, Union institution, body, office, or agency, or a person acting on their behalf [Art. 49(3)], [Art. 26(8)]. 

This is not legal advice.

Under the EU AI Act, a quality management system put in place by a provider of a high-risk AI system must be documented in written policies, procedures, and instructions, and specifically address development and quality through:

* "techniques, procedures and systematic actions to be used for the development, quality control and quality assurance of the high-risk AI system" [Art. 17(1)(c)];
* "examination, test and validation procedures to be carried out before, during and after the development of the high-risk AI system, and the frequency with which they have to be carried out" [Art. 17(1)(d)];
* "techniques, procedures and systematic actions to be used for the design, design control and design verification of the high-risk AI system" [Art. 17(1)(b)].

Under the EU AI Act, the mandate of an authorised representative for a high-risk AI system must empower them to verify that:

* The EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 has been drawn up [Art. 22(3)(a)];
* The technical documentation referred to in Article 11 has been drawn up [Art. 22(3)(a)];
* An appropriate conformity assessment procedure has been carried out by the provider [Art. 22(3)(a)].

Additionally, where the registration is carried out by the provider itself, the authorised representative must ensure that the information referred to in point 3 of Section A of Annex VIII is correct (or, where applicable, comply with the registration obligations referred to in Article 49(1)) [Art. 22(3)(e)].

Yes. Any information or documentation obtained by national public authorities or bodies protecting fundamental rights under Article 77 must be treated in accordance with the confidentiality obligations set out in Article 78 [Art. 77(4)]. 

Specifically, they must, in accordance with Union or national law, respect the confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out their tasks and activities to protect [Art. 78(1)]:
* intellectual property rights, confidential business information, or trade secrets (including source code), except as provided in Article 5 of Directive (EU) 2016/943 [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* the effective implementation of the Regulation, particularly for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* the conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* information classified under Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

A general-purpose AI model is classified as a general-purpose AI model with systemic risk if it meets either of the following conditions [Art. 51(1)]:

* **High impact capabilities:** Evaluated on the basis of appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks [Art. 51(1)(a)]. A model is presumed to meet this condition when the cumulative amount of computation used for its training exceeds $10^{25}$ floating point operations [Art. 51(2)].
* **Commission designation:** Decided by the Commission (ex officio or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel) because the model has capabilities or an impact equivalent to high impact capabilities, assessed against the criteria in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Art. 52(4)].

Under Annex XIII, the criteria considered for determining equivalent capabilities or impact are [Annex XIII]:
* **Parameters:** The number of parameters of the model [Annex XIII(a)];
* **Data set:** The quality or size of the data set, such as measured through tokens [Annex XIII(b)];
* **Computation:** The amount of computation used for training, measured in floating point operations or indicated by variables such as estimated training cost, time required, or energy consumption [Annex XIII(c)];
* **Modalities:** The input and output modalities (e.g., text-to-text, text-to-image, multi-modality), state-of-the-art thresholds for each modality, and specific input/output types (e.g., biological sequences) [Annex XIII(d)];
* **Capability benchmarks and evaluations:** Factors including the number of tasks performed without additional training, adaptability to learn new and distinct tasks, level of autonomy, scalability, and tools the model can access [Annex XIII(e)];
* **Market reach:** Whether the model has a high impact on the internal market due to reach, which is presumed if made available to at least 10,000 registered business users established in the Union [Annex XIII(f)];
* **End-users:** The number of registered end-users [Annex XIII(g)].

Upon request by a competent authority, providers of high-risk AI systems must make available:

* **Conformity information and documentation:** All information and documentation necessary to demonstrate the conformity of the system with the requirements set out in Section 2, upon a reasoned request [Art. 21(1)], [Art. 16(k)]. This must be provided in a language easily understood by the authority, in one of the official languages of the Union institutions as indicated by the Member State concerned [Art. 21(1)].
* **Automatically generated logs:** Access to the automatically generated logs of the AI system referred to in Article 12(1), upon a reasoned request and as applicable, to the extent such logs are under the provider's control [Art. 21(2)].
* **EU declaration of conformity:** A copy of the written machine-readable, physical, or electronically signed EU declaration of conformity upon request [Art. 47(1)].

Additionally, providers must keep the following at the disposal of national competent authorities for 10 years after the system is placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)], [Art. 47(1)]:
* Technical documentation [Art. 18(1)(a)];
* Quality management system documentation [Art. 18(1)(b)];
* Documentation on changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
* Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
* The EU declaration of conformity [Art. 18(1)(e)], [Art. 47(1)].

Under the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, participants in the standardisation process must seek to achieve the following goals [Art. 40(3)]:

* **Promote investment and innovation in AI**, including through increasing legal certainty [Art. 40(3)];
* **Promote the competitiveness and growth of the Union market** [Art. 40(3)];
* **Strengthen global cooperation on standardisation**, taking into account existing international standards in the field of AI that are consistent with Union values, fundamental rights, and interests [Art. 40(3)];
* **Enhance multi-stakeholder governance**, ensuring a balanced representation of interests and the effective participation of all relevant stakeholders pursuant to Articles 5, 6, and 7 of Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 [Art. 40(3)].

Additionally, European standardisation organisations tasked through Commission requests must aim to develop standards that are clear and consistent, aiming to ensure that high-risk AI systems and general-purpose AI models meet the relevant requirements and obligations of the Regulation, as well as deliver reporting and documentation processes to improve resource performance (such as reducing lifecycle energy and resource consumption) and support energy-efficient model development [Art. 40(2)].

Under the EU AI Act, at the request of the Commission or on its own initiative, the Board may issue recommendations and written opinions on any relevant matters related to the implementation of the Regulation and to its consistent and effective application [Art. 66(e)]. In particular, this includes:

* The development and application of codes of conduct and codes of practice pursuant to the Regulation, as well as the Commission’s guidelines [Art. 66(e)(i)];
* The evaluation and review of the Regulation pursuant to Article 112, including regarding serious incident reports (Article 73), the functioning of the EU database (Article 71), the preparation of delegated or implementing acts, and possible alignments with the Union harmonisation legislation listed in Annex I [Art. 66(e)(ii)];
* Technical specifications or existing standards regarding the requirements set out in Chapter III, Section 2 [Art. 66(e)(iii)];
* The use of harmonised standards or common specifications referred to in Articles 40 and 41 [Art. 66(e)(iv)];
* Trends such as European global competitiveness in AI, the uptake of AI in the Union, and the development of digital skills [Art. 66(e)(v)];
* Trends on the evolving typology of AI value chains, in particular on the resulting implications in terms of accountability [Art. 66(e)(vi)];
* The potential need for amendments to Annex III in accordance with Article 7, and for the possible revision of Article 5 pursuant to Article 112, taking into account relevant available evidence and the latest developments in technology [Art. 66(e)(vii)].

Additionally, the Board may provide opinions to the Commission on qualified alerts regarding general-purpose AI models [Art. 66(n)].

According to the EU AI Act, the instructions for use for a high-risk AI system must contain information on the human oversight measures referred to in Article 14, including the technical measures put in place to facilitate the interpretation of the system's outputs by deployers [Art. 13(3)(d)].

The oversight measures set out in Article 14 include:

* **Types of measures:** Measures identified and built into the AI system by the provider, and/or measures identified by the provider that are appropriate to be implemented by the deployer; these must be commensurate with the risks, level of autonomy, and context of use [Art. 14(3)].
* **Enabling oversight personnel:** Measures that enable the natural persons assigned to human oversight, as appropriate and proportionate:
  * to properly understand the capacities and limitations of the system and duly monitor its operation (including detecting and addressing anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance) [Art. 14(4)(a)];
  * to remain aware of automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on the system's output) [Art. 14(4)(b)];
  * to correctly interpret the system's output using available interpretation tools and methods [Art. 14(4)(c)];
  * to decide not to use the system or to disregard, override, or reverse its output [Art. 14(4)(d)];
  * to intervene or interrupt the system through a "stop" button or similar procedure to bring it to a halt in a safe state [Art. 14(4)(e)].
* **Dual-verification for specific systems:** For high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, oversight measures must ensure that no action or decision is taken on the basis of the system's identification unless it has been separately verified and confirmed by at least two natural persons with the necessary competence, training, and authority (unless Union or national law considers this disproportionate for law enforcement, migration, border control, or asylum) [Art. 14(5)].

Human oversight measures must aim to prevent or minimise risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights emerging from the system's intended purpose or reasonably foreseeable misuse [Art. 14(2)].

Under Article 66(j), the Board assists national competent authorities and the Commission by:

* Assisting them in "developing the organisational and technical expertise required for the implementation of this Regulation" [Art. 66(j)];
* "contributing to the assessment of training needs for staff of Member States involved in implementing this Regulation" [Art. 66(j)].

Under Article 10(5), providers of high-risk AI systems may process special categories of personal data only "exceptionally" and "to the extent that it is strictly necessary for the purpose of ensuring bias detection and correction" in relation to high-risk AI systems, subject to appropriate safeguards for natural persons' fundamental rights and freedoms [Art. 10(5)]. 

In addition to compliance with Regulations (EU) 2016/679 and (EU) 2018/1725 and Directive (EU) 2016/680, such processing is permitted only if all of the following conditions are met [Art. 10(5)]:

* The bias detection and correction cannot be effectively fulfilled by processing other data, including synthetic or anonymised data [Art. 10(5)];
* The data are subject to technical limitations on re-use as well as state-of-the-art security and privacy-preserving measures, including pseudonymisation [Art. 10(5)];
* The data are secured, protected, and subject to suitable safeguards—including strict access controls and documentation—to prevent misuse and ensure access is restricted to authorised persons under appropriate confidentiality obligations [Art. 10(5)];
* The data are not transmitted, transferred, or otherwise accessed by other parties [Art. 10(5)];
* The data are deleted once the bias has been corrected or the personal data reaches the end of its retention period, whichever occurs first [Art. 10(5)];
* The records of processing activities document the reasons why the processing was strictly necessary to detect and correct biases, and why that objective could not be achieved by processing other data [Art. 10(5)].

Where a high-risk AI system does not use techniques involving the training of AI models, these requirements apply only to the testing data sets [Art. 10(6)].

Under the EU AI Act, high-risk AI systems must technically allow for the "automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system" [Art. 12(1)]. 

To ensure an appropriate level of traceability, logging capabilities must enable the recording of events relevant for:
* "identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification" [Art. 12(2)(a)];
* "facilitating the post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72" [Art. 12(2)(b)]; and
* "monitoring the operation of high-risk AI systems referred to in Article 26(5)" [Art. 12(2)(c)].

Additionally, for high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1 (a) of Annex III, the logging capabilities must provide, at a minimum, the recording of:
* "the period of each use of the system (start date and time and end date and time of each use)" [Art. 12(3)(a)];
* "the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system" [Art. 12(3)(b)];
* "the input data for which the search has led to a match" [Art. 12(3)(c)]; and
* "the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results, as referred to in Article 14(5)" [Art. 12(3)(d)].

Yes. The Commission must ensure that "all sensitive information obtained in the course of its investigations" into the competence of a notified body is "treated confidentially in accordance with Article 78" [Art. 37(3)].

Under these confidentiality rules, the Commission must, in accordance with Union or national law, respect the confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out its tasks so as to protect, in particular [Art. 78(1)]:

* Intellectual property rights, confidential business information, or trade secrets, including source code (except in cases referred to in Article 5 of Directive (EU) 2016/943) [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* The effective implementation of the Regulation, specifically for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* Public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* The conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* Information classified pursuant to Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

Under Annex IV, point 3, the technical documentation must contain detailed information regarding the monitoring, functioning, and control of the AI system, specifically concerning [Annex IV, point 3]:

* **Capabilities and limitations in performance:** including the degrees of accuracy for specific persons or groups of persons on which the system is intended to be used, as well as the overall expected level of accuracy relative to its intended purpose [Annex IV, point 3];
* **Foreseeable unintended outcomes and risks:** sources of risks to health and safety, fundamental rights, and discrimination in view of the intended purpose of the AI system [Annex IV, point 3];
* **Human oversight measures:** measures needed in accordance with Article 14, including technical measures implemented to facilitate the interpretation of the AI system’s outputs by deployers [Annex IV, point 3];
* **Input data:** specifications on input data, as appropriate [Annex IV, point 3].

In addition, the documentation must include:
* A description of the appropriateness of the performance metrics for the specific AI system [Annex IV, points 4–6];
* A detailed description of the risk management system in accordance with Article 9 [Annex IV, points 4–6];
* A description of relevant changes made by the provider to the system throughout its lifecycle [Annex IV, points 4–6].

SMEs, including start-ups, may provide the elements of Annex IV in a simplified manner using a form established by the Commission [Art. 11(1)].

Yes. Notifying authorities are required to safeguard the confidentiality of the information that they obtain, in accordance with Article 78 [Art. 28(6)]. 

Under these rules, confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out tasks and activities must be respected in accordance with Union or national law, in particular to protect [Art. 78(1)]:
* intellectual property rights and confidential business information or trade secrets of a natural or legal person, including source code (except in cases referred to in Article 5 of Directive (EU) 2016/943) [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* the effective implementation of the Regulation, particularly for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* the conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* information classified pursuant to Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

However, the notifying authority is required to provide the Commission, on request, with all relevant information relating to the notification or the maintenance of the competence of the notified body concerned [Art. 37(2)]. (The Commission must likewise ensure that sensitive information obtained during such investigations is treated confidentially [Art. 37(3)]).

Under Article 59(1)(i) of the EU AI Act, the required documentation is:

* "a complete and detailed description of the process and rationale behind the training, testing and validation of the AI system" [Art. 59(1)(i)];
* this description must be "kept together with the testing results as part of the technical documentation referred to in Annex IV" [Art. 59(1)(i)].

If a provider or its authorised representative established on a Member State's territory goes bankrupt or ceases its activity prior to the end of the required storage period, each Member State must determine the conditions under which the documentation remains at the disposal of the national competent authorities for that period [Art. 18(2)].

This applies to the documentation that high-risk AI system providers must otherwise keep at the disposal of national competent authorities for a period ending 10 years after the system has been placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1), Art. 18(2)], namely:
* The technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
* The quality management system documentation referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
* Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
* Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
* The EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)].

Based on the provided excerpts, the European Commission is assisted and advised by:

*   **The European Artificial Intelligence Board (the Board):** The Board advises and assists the Commission and Member States to facilitate the consistent and effective application of the Regulation [Art. 66(e)], [Art. 66(m)], [Art. 66(f)], [Art. 66(c)]. Specifically, the Board may:
    *   Issue recommendations and written opinions on relevant implementation matters at the request of the Commission or on its own initiative [Art. 66(e)];
    *   Advise the Commission regarding international matters on AI [Art. 66(m)];
    *   Support the Commission in promoting AI literacy, public awareness, and understanding of the benefits, risks, safeguards, rights, and obligations concerning AI systems [Art. 66(f)];
    *   Provide advice on implementation, particularly regarding the enforcement of rules on general-purpose AI models [Art. 66(c)].

*   **Independent Experts and the Scientific Panel:** The Commission may appoint independent experts to carry out evaluations on its behalf, including experts from the scientific panel established under Article 68 [Art. 92(2)]. To be appointed, these experts must:
    *   Have particular expertise, competence, and scientific or technical expertise in the field of AI [Art. 68(2)(a)], [Art. 92(2)];
    *   Be independent from any provider of AI systems or general-purpose AI models [Art. 68(2)(b)], [Art. 92(2)];
    *   Demonstrate the ability to perform activities diligently, accurately, and objectively [Art. 68(2)(c)], [Art. 92(2)].

When the Commission requests information from a provider of a general-purpose AI model under Article 91, the request for information must [Art. 91(1)], [Art. 91(4)]:

* state the legal basis of the request [Art. 91(4)];
* state the purpose of the request [Art. 91(4)];
* specify what information is required [Art. 91(4)];
* set a period within which the information is to be provided [Art. 91(4)]; and
* indicate the fines provided for in Article 101 for supplying incorrect, incomplete or misleading information [Art. 91(4)].

The information that may be requested includes the documentation drawn up by the provider in accordance with Articles 53 and 55, or any additional information necessary for assessing the provider's compliance with the Regulation [Art. 91(1)].

Yes. When performing their tasks, national competent authorities must act in accordance with the confidentiality obligations set out in Article 78 [Art. 70(5)]. 

Specifically, market surveillance authorities and any other person involved in applying the Regulation must, in accordance with Union or national law, respect the confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out their tasks and activities, in order to protect [Art. 78(1)]:
* intellectual property rights, confidential business information, or trade secrets, including source code (except as provided in Article 5 of Directive (EU) 2016/943) [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* the effective implementation of the Regulation, particularly for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* the conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* information classified under Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

Additionally:
* National public authorities or bodies protecting fundamental rights must treat information or documentation obtained under Article 77 in accordance with the Article 78 confidentiality obligations [Art. 77(4)].
* Information exchanged on a confidential basis between national competent authorities, or between them and the Commission, cannot be disclosed without prior consultation of the originating authority and the deployer when high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1, 6, or 7 of Annex III are used by law enforcement, border control, immigration, or asylum authorities and disclosure would jeopardize public and national security interests [Art. 78(3)].
* Such information exchanges must not cover sensitive operational data, and technical documentation of certain law enforcement, immigration, or asylum systems must remain on their premises and only be accessed by market surveillance staff holding the appropriate security clearance [Art. 78(3)].

According to Article 1(1), the purpose of the Regulation is to:

* "improve the functioning of the internal market" [Art. 1(1)];
* "promote the uptake of human-centric and trustworthy artificial intelligence (AI)" [Art. 1(1)];
* ensure "a high level of protection of health, safety, fundamental rights enshrined in the Charter, including democracy, the rule of law and environmental protection, against the harmful effects of AI systems in the Union" [Art. 1(1)]; and
* support innovation [Art. 1(1)].

Prior to participating in testing in real world conditions, subjects must be duly given concise, clear, relevant, and understandable information regarding [Art. 61(1)]:

* **Nature and objectives:** The nature and objectives of the testing, as well as the possible inconvenience that may be linked to their participation [Art. 61(1)];
* **Conditions and duration:** The conditions under which the testing will be conducted, including the expected duration of the subject's participation [Art. 61(1)];
* **Rights and guarantees:** Their rights and guarantees, in particular the right to refuse to participate and the right to withdraw from testing at any time without any resulting detriment and without having to provide any justification [Art. 61(1)];
* **Reversal arrangements:** The arrangements for requesting the reversal or the disregarding of the predictions, recommendations, or decisions of the AI system [Art. 61(1)];
* **Identification and contacts:** The Union-wide unique single identification number of the real-world testing, and the contact details of the provider or its legal representative from whom further information can be obtained [Art. 61(1)].

More generally, informed consent requires that the subject is informed of all aspects of the testing that are relevant to their decision to participate [Art. 3(59)]. Once consent is given, it must be dated and documented, and a copy must be provided to the subjects or their legal representative [Art. 61(2)].

Notifying authorities notify the Commission and other Member States of conformity assessment bodies using the electronic notification tool developed and managed by the Commission [Art. 30(2)].

The notification must include:
* Full details of the conformity assessment activities [Art. 30(3)];
* The conformity assessment module or modules [Art. 30(3)];
* The types of AI systems concerned [Art. 30(3)];
* The relevant attestation of competence [Art. 30(3)].

Where the notification is not based on an accreditation certificate as referred to in Article 29(2), the notifying authority must also provide documentary evidence attesting to the competence of the body and the arrangements in place to ensure regular monitoring and continued compliance with Article 31 [Art. 30(3)].

The conformity assessment body may only perform the activities of a notified body if no objections are raised by the Commission or other Member States within:
* Two weeks, where the notification includes an accreditation certificate [Art. 30(4)]; or
* Two months, where it includes documentary evidence [Art. 30(4)].

Under Annex XII, providers of general-purpose AI models must provide technical documentation containing at least the following details to downstream providers [Annex XII]:

**1. A general description of the general-purpose AI model:**
* The tasks the model is intended to perform and the type and nature of AI systems into which it can be integrated [Annex XII];
* The applicable acceptable use policies [Annex XII];
* The date of release and methods of distribution [Annex XII];
* How the model interacts, or can be used to interact, with hardware or software that is not part of the model itself, where applicable [Annex XII];
* The versions of relevant software related to the use of the model, where applicable [Annex XII];
* The architecture and number of parameters [Annex XII];
* The modality (e.g. text, image) and format of inputs and outputs [Annex XII];
* The licence for the model [Annex XII].

**2. A description of the elements of the model and the process for its development:**
* The technical means (such as instructions for use, infrastructure, tools) required for the model to be integrated into AI systems [Annex XII];
* The modality and format of inputs and outputs and their maximum size (e.g. context window length) [Annex XII];
* Information on the data used for training, testing, and validation, where applicable, including data type, provenance,

Under Annex X, point 1 of the AI Act, the Union legislative acts covering the Schengen Information System are [Annex X, point 1]:

* **Regulation (EU) 2018/1860** on the use of the Schengen Information System for the return of illegally staying third-country nationals [Annex X, point 1];
* **Regulation (EU) 2018/1861** on the establishment, operation and use of the Schengen Information System (SIS) in the field of border checks [Annex X, point 1];
* **Regulation (EU) 2018/1862** on the establishment, operation and use of the Schengen Information System (SIS) in the field of police cooperation and judicial cooperation in criminal matters [Annex X, point 1].

Under the EU AI Act, a complaint lodged by a downstream provider alleging an infringement of the Regulation must be duly reasoned and indicate at least the following [Art. 89(2)]:

* The point of contact of the provider of the general-purpose AI model concerned [Art. 89(2)(a)];
* A description of the relevant facts, the provisions of the Regulation concerned, and the reason why the downstream provider considers that the provider of the general-purpose AI model concerned infringed the Regulation [Art. 89(2)(b)];
* Any other information that the downstream provider considers relevant, including, where appropriate, information gathered on its own initiative [Art. 89(2)(c)].

An authorised representative must keep the documentation for a high-risk AI system at the disposal of the competent authorities and relevant national authorities or bodies for **a period of 10 years after the high-risk AI system has been placed on the market or put into service** [Art. 22(3)(b)].

Under the required mandate, the documentation to be kept during this 10-year period includes [Art. 22(3)(b)]:
* the contact details of the provider that appointed the authorised representative;
* a copy of the EU declaration of conformity;
* the technical documentation; and
* if applicable, the certificate issued by the notified body.

Based on the provided excerpts:

* **Conformity assessment bodies (assessment and monitoring):** Member States may decide that the assessment and monitoring of conformity assessment bodies is carried out by a national accreditation body within the meaning of, and in accordance with, Regulation (EC) No 765/2008 [Art. 28(2)]. Furthermore, an application for notification by a conformity assessment body may be accompanied by an accreditation certificate issued by a national accreditation body [Art. 29(2)]. However, the excerpts do not state that the national accreditation body itself carries out conformity assessments.
* **Market surveillance:** The provided provisions do not address or authorize designating a national accreditation body to handle market surveillance. The excerpts instead provide that market surveillance authorities are:
  * For certain Annex I products, the authorities responsible under that Union harmonisation legislation, or another relevant authority provided coordination is ensured [Art. 74(3)];
  * For specific high-risk AI systems listed in Annex III, competent data protection supervisory authorities or other authorities under Directive (EU) 2016/680 [Art. 74(8)];
  * Designated to act as the single point of contact and communicated to the Commission [Art. 70(2)].

Under Article 1(2), the AI Act lays down:
* Harmonised rules for the placing on the market, putting into service, and use of AI systems in the Union [Art. 1(2)];
* Prohibitions of certain AI practices [Art. 1(2)];
* Specific requirements for high-risk AI systems and obligations for operators of such systems [Art. 1(2)];
* Harmonised transparency rules for certain AI systems [Art. 1(2)];
* Harmonised rules for the placing on the market of general-purpose AI models [Art. 1(2)];
* Rules on market monitoring, market surveillance, governance, and enforcement [Art. 1(2)];
* Measures to support innovation, with a particular focus on SMEs, including start-ups [Art. 1(2)].

These rules follow a risk-based approach that tailors the type and content of the rules to the intensity and scope of the risks that AI systems can generate [Recital 26]. In addition, specific innovation support measures include actions by the AI Office to provide standardised templates, maintain a single information platform, organise awareness-raising communication campaigns, and promote best practices in public procurement [Art. 62(3)].

Under Article 90(3), a qualified alert issued by the scientific panel must be "duly reasoned" and indicate at least the following information [Art. 90(3)]:

* the point of contact of the provider of the general-purpose AI model with systemic risk concerned [Art. 90(3)(a)];
* a description of the relevant facts and the reasons for the alert by the scientific panel [Art. 90(3)(b)];
* any other information that the scientific panel considers to be relevant, including, where appropriate, information gathered on its own initiative [Art. 90(3)(c)].

Yes. Under the AI Act, in order to advise and assist the Commission and the Member States to facilitate the consistent and effective application of the Regulation, the European Artificial Intelligence Board may in particular:

* "contribute to effective cooperation with the competent authorities of third countries and with international organisations" [Art. 66(i)];
* "advise the Commission in relation to international matters on AI" [Art. 66(m)].

Yes. Member States and the Commission may exchange confidential information with regulatory authorities of third countries, subject to the following conditions [Art. 78(5)]:

* The exchange must be "where necessary" [Art. 78(5)];
* It must be "in accordance with relevant provisions of international and trade agreements" [Art. 78(5)];
* It must be conducted with regulatory authorities with which they have concluded "bilateral or multilateral confidentiality arrangements guaranteeing an adequate level of confidentiality" [Art. 78(5)].

When deciding on the amount of an administrative fine, authorities must take into account all relevant circumstances of the specific situation, giving regard to particular factors depending on the entity involved:

**For operators in general**, authorities consider [Art. 99(7)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, including the purpose of the AI system, the number of affected persons, and the level of damage suffered [Art. 99(7)(a)];
* Whether administrative fines have already been applied to the same operator for the same infringement by other market surveillance authorities [Art. 99(7)(b)], or for infringements of other Union or national law resulting from the same activity or omission [Art. 99(7)(c)];
* The size, annual turnover, and market share of the operator [Art. 99(7)(d)];
* Any other aggravating or mitigating factors, including financial benefits gained or losses avoided directly or indirectly [Art. 99(7)(e)];
* The degree of cooperation with national competent authorities to remedy the infringement and mitigate adverse effects [Art. 99(7)(f)];
* The degree of responsibility, taking into account implemented technical and organisational measures [Art. 99(7)(g)];
* How the infringement became known to the authorities, particularly whether and to what extent it was notified by the operator [Art. 99(7)(h)];
* The intentional or negligent character of the infringement [Art. 99(7)(i)];
* Any action taken by the operator to mitigate harm suffered by affected persons [Art. 99(7)(j)].

**For Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies**, the European Data Protection Supervisor considers [Art. 100(1)]:
* The nature, gravity, duration, and consequences of the infringement, the purpose of the AI system, the number of affected persons, and the level of damage [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* The degree of responsibility, accounting for technical and organisational measures [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* Actions taken to mitigate damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* The degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor, including compliance with previously ordered measures regarding the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* Any similar previous infringements [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* The manner in which the infringement became known, particularly whether and to what extent it was notified [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* The annual budget of the institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

**For providers of general-purpose AI models**, the Commission considers [Art. 101(1)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement, taking due account of proportionality and appropriateness [Art. 101(1)];
* Commitments made in accordance with Article 93(3) or in relevant codes of practice in accordance with Article 56 [Art. 101(1)].

Under Annex IX, the following information must be provided and thereafter kept up to date when registering testing in real-world conditions for a high-risk AI system [Annex IX]:

* A Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing in real-world conditions [Annex IX];
* The name and contact details of the provider or prospective provider and of the deployers involved in the testing in real-world conditions [Annex IX];
* A brief description of the AI system, its intended purpose, and other information necessary for the identification of the system [Annex IX];
* A summary of the main characteristics of the plan for testing in real-world conditions [Annex IX];
* Information on the suspension or termination of the testing in real-world conditions [Annex IX].

This is not legal advice.

Under Annex VIII, Section B, points 1 to 6 of the AI Act, providers registering AI systems in accordance with Article 49(2) must provide—and thereafter keep up to date—the following details [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6]:

1. The name, address and contact details of the provider [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
2. Where the submission of information is carried out by another person on behalf of the provider, the name, address and contact details of that person [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
3. The name, address and contact details of the authorised representative, where applicable [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
4. The AI system trade name and any additional unambiguous reference allowing the identification and traceability of the AI system [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
5. A description of the intended purpose of the AI system [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
6. The condition or conditions under Article 6(3) based on which the AI system is considered to be not-high-risk [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6].

This is not legal advice.

Yes, the European Commission is required to develop guidelines on the practical implementation of the AI Act, and it must specifically consider small businesses [Art. 96(1)].

**Practical Guidelines from the Commission**
* The Commission must develop guidelines on practical implementation, in particular regarding [Art. 96(1)]:
  * the requirements and obligations in Articles 8 to 15 and Article 25 [Art. 96(1)(a)];
  * prohibited practices under Article 5 [Art. 96(1)(b)];
  * substantial modification provisions [Art. 96(1)(c)];
  * transparency obligations under Article 50 [Art. 96(1)(d)];
  * the relationship with Union harmonisation legislation in Annex I and other Union law [Art. 96(1)(e)];
  * the application of the definition of an AI system [Art. 96(1)(f)].
* After consulting the European Artificial Intelligence Board, the Commission must also provide guidelines by no later than 2 February 2026 specifying the practical implementation of high-risk classification rules, "together with a comprehensive list of practical examples of use cases of AI systems that are high-risk and not high-risk" [Art. 6(5)]. Later amendments to the Act are not reflected in this text.

**Consideration of Small Businesses**
* When issuing its general practical guidelines, the Commission must "pay particular attention to the needs of SMEs including start-ups" [Art. 96(1)].
* For qualifying microenterprises, the Commission must develop guidelines detailing the elements of the quality management system that may be fulfilled in a simplified manner, "considering the needs of microenterprises" [Art. 63(1)], [Recital 146].
* The Commission is also required to establish a simplified technical documentation form specifically "targeted at the needs of small and microenterprises" [Art. 11(1)].

Before placing a high-risk AI system on the market, importers must ensure that the system conforms with the Regulation by verifying that:

* the provider has carried out the relevant conformity assessment procedure referred to in Article 43 [Art. 23(1)];
* the provider has drawn up the technical documentation in accordance with Article 11 and Annex IV [Art. 23(1)];
* the system bears the required CE marking and is accompanied by the instructions for use and the EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 23(1)];
* the provider has appointed an authorised representative in accordance with Article 22(1) [Art. 23(1)].

If an importer has sufficient reason to consider that the system is not in conformity, is falsified, or is accompanied by falsified documentation, it must not place the system on the market until it has been brought into conformity [Art. 23(2)].

Under Annex IV, point 1 of the EU AI Act, the technical documentation must contain a general description of the AI system, as applicable to the relevant AI system [Annex IV], including:

* Its intended purpose, the name of the provider, and the version of the system reflecting its relation to previous versions [Annex IV, point 1(a)];
* How the AI system interacts with, or can be used to interact with, hardware or software (including other AI systems) that are not part of the AI system itself, where applicable [Annex IV, point 1(b)];
* The versions of relevant software or firmware, and any requirements related to version updates [Annex IV, point 1(c)];
* A description of all the forms in which the AI system is placed on the market or put into service, such as software packages embedded into hardware, downloads, or APIs [Annex IV, point 1(d)];
* A description of the hardware on which the AI system is intended to run [Annex IV, point 1(e)];
* Where the AI system is a component of products, photographs or illustrations showing external features, marking, and the internal layout of those products [Annex IV, point 1(f)];
* A basic description of the user-interface provided to the deployer [Annex IV, point 1(g)];
* Instructions for use for the deployer, and a basic description of the user-interface provided to the deployer, where applicable [Annex IV, point 1(h)].

Under Article 93(1), where "necessary and appropriate", the Commission may request providers to take the following actions [Art. 93(1)]:

* "take appropriate measures to comply with the obligations set out in Articles 53 and 54" [Art. 93(1)(a)];
* "implement mitigation measures, where the evaluation carried out in accordance with Article 92 has given rise to serious and substantiated concern of a systemic risk at Union level" [Art. 93(1)(b)];
* "restrict the making available on the market, withdraw or recall the model" [Art. 93(1)(c)].

Under the EU AI Act, high-risk AI systems that use techniques involving the training of AI models must be developed using training, validation, and testing data sets that meet specific data quality and governance criteria [Art. 10(1)]. For systems not using training techniques, these data requirements apply only to testing data sets [Art. 10(6)].

### Data Governance and Management Practices
Training, validation, and testing data sets must be subject to data governance and management practices appropriate to the system's intended purpose, covering [Art. 10(2)]:
* Relevant design choices [Art. 10(2)(a)];
* Data collection processes, data origin, and—in the case of personal data—the original purpose of the collection [Art. 10(2)(b)];
* Data-preparation operations, including annotation, labelling, cleaning, updating, enrichment, and aggregation [Art. 10(2)(c)];
* Formulation of assumptions, especially regarding what the data is intended to measure and represent [Art. 10(2)(d)];
* Assessment of the availability, quantity, and suitability of the required data sets [Art. 10(2)(e)];
* Examination of possible biases likely to affect health and safety, negatively impact fundamental rights, or lead to discrimination prohibited under Union law, particularly where outputs influence future inputs [Art. 10(2)(f)];
* Appropriate measures to detect, prevent, and mitigate those identified biases [Art. 10(2)(g)];
* Identification of data gaps or shortcomings that prevent compliance, and how to address them [Art. 10(2)(h)].

### Data Quality Requirements
* **Quality criteria:** Data sets must be relevant, sufficiently representative, and to the best extent possible, free of errors and complete in view of the intended purpose [Art. 10(3)].
* **Statistical properties:** They must have appropriate statistical properties, including regarding the persons or groups of persons the system is intended to be used on; these may be met across individual or combined data sets [Art. 10(3)].
* **Contextual setting:** Data sets must take into account characteristics specific to the geographical, contextual, behavioural, or functional setting where the system will be used [Art. 10(4)].

### Processing Special Categories of Personal Data for Bias Mitigation
Providers may exceptionally process special categories of personal data strictly when necessary to detect and correct bias, subject to fundamental rights safeguards and all of the following conditions [Art. 10(5)]:
* The bias detection and correction cannot effectively be achieved using other data, such as synthetic or anonymised data [Art. 10(5)(a)];
* The data is subject to technical limitations on re-use and state-of-the-art security and privacy-preserving measures (including pseudonymisation) [Art. 10(5)(b)];
* Strict access controls, documentation, and confidentiality measures are applied to prevent misuse [Art. 10(5)(c)];
* The data is not transmitted, transferred, or accessed by other parties [Art. 10(5)(d)];
* The data is deleted once the bias is corrected or the retention period ends, whichever is first [Art. 10(5)(e)];
* Processing records state the reasons why processing such data was strictly necessary and why other data could not achieve the objective [Art. 10(5)(f)].

### Quality Management and Deployer Obligations
* **Quality management system:** Providers must document systems and procedures for data management (e.g., acquisition, collection, analysis, labelling, storage, filtration, mining, aggregation, and retention performed prior to placing on the market or putting into service) within their quality management system [Art. 17(1)(f)].
* **Deployer obligations:** To the extent deployers exercise control over input

Under the AI Act, a ‘distributor’ is:

* "a natural or legal person in the supply chain, other than the provider or the importer, that makes an AI system available on the Union market" [Art. 3(7)].

However, a distributor is considered to be a provider of a high-risk AI system (and subject to provider obligations) under any of the following circumstances:
* they put their name or trademark on a high-risk AI system already placed on the market or put into service, without prejudice to contractual arrangements stipulating that obligations are otherwise allocated [Art. 25(1)(a)];
* they make a substantial modification to an already placed on the market or put into service high-risk AI system such that it remains high-risk [Art. 25(1)(b)];
* they modify the intended purpose of an AI system (including a general-purpose AI system) not classified as high-risk and already placed on the market or put into service in such a way that it becomes a high-risk AI system [Art. 25(1)(c)].

Under the AI Act, a ‘real-world testing plan’ means a document that describes the following elements regarding testing in real-world conditions [Art. 3(53)]:

* objectives;
* methodology;
* geographical, population, and temporal scope;
* monitoring;
* organisation; and
* conduct of testing [Art. 3(53)].

Additionally, the Commission is tasked with specifying the detailed elements of the real-world testing plan by means of implementing acts [Art. 60(1)].

Under the AI Act, ‘AI literacy’ means "skills, knowledge and understanding that allow providers, deployers and affected persons, taking into account their respective rights and obligations in the context of this Regulation, to make an informed deployment of AI systems, as well as to gain awareness about the opportunities and risks of AI and possible harm it can cause" [Art. 3(56)].

Recital 20 further clarifies that AI literacy should equip these parties with the notions needed to "make informed decisions regarding AI systems," which can include [Recital 20]:
* understanding the correct application of technical elements during the development phase [Recital 20];
* understanding the measures to apply during use and the suitable ways to interpret the AI system's output [Recital 20];
* for affected persons, the knowledge necessary to understand how decisions assisted by AI will impact them [Recital 20]; and
* providing actors across the AI value chain with the insights needed to ensure appropriate compliance and enforcement [Recital 20].

Under the AI Act, a ‘publicly accessible space’ means:

* any publicly or privately owned physical place accessible to an undetermined number of natural persons [Art. 3(44)];
* this applies regardless of whether certain conditions for access may apply, and regardless of potential capacity restrictions [Art. 3(44)].

Under the AI Act, a ‘serious incident’ means an incident or malfunctioning of an AI system that directly or indirectly leads to any of the following [Art. 3(49)]:

* "the death of a person, or serious harm to a person’s health" [Art. 3(49)];
* "a serious and irreversible disruption of the management or operation of critical infrastructure" [Art. 3(49)];
* "the infringement of obligations under Union law intended to protect fundamental rights" [Art. 3(49)];
* "serious harm to property or the environment" [Art. 3(49)].

Under the AI Act, an ‘operator’ means any of the following [Art. 3(8)]:

* a provider [Art. 3(8)];
* a product manufacturer [Art. 3(8)];
* a deployer [Art. 3(8)];
* an authorised representative [Art. 3(8)];
* an importer [Art. 3(8)]; or
* a distributor [Art. 3(8)].

Among these, the provided excerpts specifically define:
* **Provider**: a natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body that develops an AI system or a general-purpose AI model, or that has one developed, and places it on the market or puts the AI system into service under its own name or trademark, whether for payment or free of charge [Art. 3(3)].
* **Deployer**: a natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body using an AI system under its authority, except when used in the course of a personal non-professional activity [Art. 3(4)].

Additionally, for the purposes of market surveillance and enforcement under Regulation (EU) 2019/1020, any reference to an economic operator is understood as including all operators identified in Article 2(1) of the AI Act [Art. 74(1)(a)].

Under the AI Act, ‘informed consent’ means a subject’s freely given, specific, unambiguous and voluntary expression of his or her willingness to participate in a particular testing in real-world conditions, after having been informed of all aspects of the testing that are relevant to the subject’s decision to participate [Art. 3(59)].

For testing in real-world conditions under Article 60, informed consent must meet the following key conditions:
* It must be obtained prior to participation, after subjects are duly informed with concise, clear, relevant, and understandable information concerning [Art. 61(1)]:
  * the nature and objectives of the testing and possible inconvenience linked to participation [Art. 61(1)(a)];
  * the conditions under which it is conducted, including expected duration [Art. 61(1)(b)];
  * their rights and guarantees, particularly the right to refuse or withdraw at any time without detriment or need for justification [Art. 61(1)(c)];
  * arrangements to request the reversal or disregarding of predictions, recommendations, or decisions made by the AI system [Art. 61(1)(d)];
  * the Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing and the contact details of the provider or its legal representative [Art. 61(1)(e)].
* It must be dated and documented, and a copy must be given to the subjects or their legal representative [Art. 61(2)].

Under the EU AI Act, individuals assigned to human oversight of high-risk AI systems must be natural persons who possess:

* "the necessary competence, training and authority, as well as the necessary support" [Art. 26(2)], [Recital 73].

Additionally, the system must be provided to the deployer in a manner that enables these individuals, as appropriate and proportionate, to:
* properly understand the system's relevant capacities and limitations and duly monitor its operation, including detecting and addressing anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance [Art. 14(4)(a)];
* remain aware of the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on the output (automation bias) [Art. 14(4)(b)];
* correctly interpret the system’s output [Art. 14(4)(c)];
* decide not to use the system or disregard, override, or reverse its output [Art. 14(4)(d)];
* intervene in the operation or interrupt the system to bring it to a halt in a safe state [Art. 14(4)(e)].

You do not have to repeat the same work if it has already been addressed:

* **Complementary relationship:** If any of the obligations for the fundamental rights impact assessment (FRIA) are already met through a data protection impact assessment (DPIA) conducted pursuant to Article 35 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR) or Article 27 of Directive (EU) 2016/680, the FRIA **shall complement** that DPIA rather than duplicate it [Art. 27(4)].
* **Remaining FRIA elements:** The deployer must still ensure that all elements required under Article 27(1) are fulfilled. To the extent they are not covered by the DPIA, the assessment must include:
  * a description of the deployer's processes in which the system will be used according to its intended purpose [Art. 27(1)(a)];
  * the period of time and frequency of intended use [Art. 27(1)(b)];
  * the categories of natural persons and groups likely to be affected [Art. 27(1)(c)];
  * specific risks of harm to those persons or groups, taking into account information provided under Article 13 [Art. 27(1)(d)];
  * the implementation of human oversight measures [Art. 27(1)(e)];
  * measures to be taken if risks materialise, including governance arrangements and complaint mechanisms [Art. 27(1)(f)].
* **Relying on prior assessments:** In similar cases, deployers may also rely on previously conducted FRIAs or existing impact assessments carried out by the provider [Art. 27(2)].
* **Notification and registration:** Following the assessment, deployers must notify the market surveillance authority of the results using the AI Office template [Art. 27(3), Art. 27(5)]. In addition, where registration under Article 49(3) applies, deployers must provide both a summary of the FRIA findings and a summary of the DPIA carried out under the GDPR or Directive (EU) 2016/680 [Annex VIII, Section C, points 4–5].

