This system must be "documented in a systematic and orderly manner in the form of written policies, procedures and instructions" and must include at least the following elements [Art. 17(1)(a)]:

* A strategy for regulatory compliance, covering conformity assessment procedures and management of modifications [Art. 17(1)(a)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for the design, design control, and design verification of the system [Art. 17(1)(b)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for development, quality control, and quality assurance [Art. 17(1)(c)];
* Systems and procedures for data management (such as data collection, labelling, storage, and retention prior to placing on the market or putting into service) [Art. 17(1)(f)];
* The risk management system referred to in Article 9 [Art. 17(1)(g)];
* The setting-up, implementation, and maintenance of a post-market monitoring system in accordance with Article 72 [Art. 17(1)(h)];
* Systems and procedures for record-keeping of relevant documentation and information [Art. 17(1)(k)];
* An accountability framework establishing the responsibilities of management and staff [Art. 17(1)(m)].

Under the EU AI Act, high-risk AI systems must be designed and developed—including with appropriate human-machine interface tools—so that they can be effectively overseen by natural persons during the period they are in use [Art. 14(1)]. 

The specific requirements include:

* **Objective:** Human oversight aims to prevent or minimise risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights emerging from use in accordance with the system's intended purpose or under reasonably foreseeable misuse [Art. 14(2)].
* **Types of measures:** Oversight measures must be commensurate with the risks, level of autonomy, and context of use, and must be ensured through either or both [Art. 14(3)]:
  * Measures built into the system by the provider before placing it on the market or putting it into service [Art. 14(3)(a)];
  * Measures identified by the provider prior to market placement or putting into service that are appropriate for the deployer to implement [Art. 14(3)(b)]. Instructions for use must state these oversight measures, including technical measures facilitating output interpretation [Art. 13(3)(d)].
* **Enabling oversight personnel:** Systems must be provided to deployers such that individuals assigned oversight are enabled, as appropriate and proportionate, to [Art. 14(4)]:
  * Understand the system's capacities and limitations, monitor its operation, and detect/address anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance [Art. 14(4)(a)];
  * Remain aware of automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on output) [Art. 14(4)(b)];
  * Correctly interpret the system's output using available interpretation tools and methods [Art. 14(4)(c)];
  * Decide not to use the system, or disregard, override, or reverse its output [Art. 14(4)(d)];
  * Intervene or halt the system safely via a "stop" button or similar procedure [Art. 14(4)(e)].
* **Deployer obligations:** Deployers must assign human oversight to natural persons who possess the necessary competence, training, authority, and support [Art. 26(2)].
* **Specific requirement for certain biometric identification systems:** For systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, no action or decision may be taken by the deployer based on the identification unless it has been separately verified and confirmed by at least two natural persons with the necessary competence, training, and authority [Art. 14(5)]. This two-person verification does not apply to law enforcement, migration, border control, or asylum use cases where Union or national law considers it disproportionate [Art. 14(5)].

Selon l'AI Act, un système d'IA est qualifié de système à haut risque dans deux cas principaux :

1. **Les systèmes liés à des produits réglementés de l'Union** [Art. 6(1)] :
Un système d'IA est considéré à haut risque, qu'il soit mis sur le marché ou mis en service indépendamment ou non de ces produits, dès lors que les deux conditions cumulatives suivantes sont remplies [Art. 6(1)] :
* Le système d'IA est destiné à être utilisé comme composant de sécurité d'un produit, ou est lui-même un produit, couvert par la législation d'harmonisation de l'Union énumérée à l'annexe I [Art. 6(1)(a)] (concernant notamment des produits tels que les machines, jouets, ascenseurs, dispositifs médicaux, etc. [Recital 50]) ;
* Ce produit (ou le système d'IA lui-même) est soumis à une évaluation de la conformité par un tiers (« third-party conformity assessment ») en vue de sa mise sur le marché ou mise en service en vertu de cette législation [Art. 6(1)(b)], [Recital 50].

2. **Les systèmes d'IA visés à l'annexe III** [Art. 6(2)], [Annex III] :
Sont également considérés à haut risque les systèmes d'IA répertoriés dans les domaines de l'annexe III [Art. 6(2)], [Annex III]. Parmi ces domaines figure notamment l'accès et le bénéfice des services privés essentiels et des services et prestations publics essentiels [Annex III, point 5] :
* Les systèmes utilisés par ou pour des autorités publiques pour évaluer l'éligibilité des personnes physiques aux prestations et services d'assistance publique essentiels (y compris les soins de santé), ainsi que pour accorder, réduire, révoquer ou récupérer ces prestations et services [Annex III, point 5](a) ;
* Les systèmes destinés à évaluer la solvabilité de personnes physiques ou à établir leur score de crédit, à l'exception des systèmes utilisés aux fins de détection des fraudes financières [Annex III, point 5](b) ;
* Les systèmes destinés à l'évaluation des risques et à la tarification pour les personnes physiques en matière d'assurance vie et santé [Annex III, point 5](c) ;
* Les systèmes destinés à évaluer et classer les appels d'urgence, à réguler ou prioriser l'envoi des services d'intervention d'urgence (police, pompiers, secours médical) ou utilisés pour le tri des patients en soins d'urgence [Annex III, point 5](d).

**Exception / procédure d'évaluation** :
Un fournisseur estimant qu'un système d'IA visé à l'annexe III n'est pas à haut risque doit documenter son évaluation avant la mise sur le marché ou en service, se soumettre à l'obligation d'enregistrement prévue à l'article 49(2) et fournir cette documentation sur demande aux autorités compétentes nationales [Art. 6(4)].

Under the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, the AI Office is the Commission’s function of contributing to the implementation, monitoring, and supervision of AI systems and general-purpose AI models, and AI governance, with references to the AI Office construed as references to the Commission [Art. 3(47)]. The Commission develops Union expertise and capabilities in the field of AI through the AI Office [Art. 64(1)].

Its specific roles and responsibilities include:

* **Market Surveillance and Enforcement:** Where an AI system is based on a general-purpose AI model and both are developed by the same provider, the AI Office has the power to monitor and supervise compliance of that AI system, holding "all the powers of a market surveillance authority provided for in this Section and Regulation (EU) 2019/1020" [Art. 75(1)].
* **Support for the European Artificial Intelligence Board:** The AI Office provides the secretariat for the Board, prepares its agenda, and convenes meetings upon request of the Chair [Art. 65(8)]. It also attends Board meetings without taking part in votes [Art. 65(2)].
* **Support Measures for Operators and Innovation:** The AI Office is required to:
  * Provide standardised templates for areas covered by the Regulation, as specified by the Board [Art. 62(3)(a)];
  * Develop and maintain a single information platform providing easy-to-use information regarding the Regulation for all operators across the Union [Art. 62(3)(b)];
  * Organise communication campaigns to raise awareness about obligations arising from the Regulation [Art. 62(3)(c)];
  * Evaluate and promote the convergence of best practices in public procurement procedures for AI systems [Art. 62(3)(d)].

By 2 August 2028, the Commission must evaluate the functioning, powers, competences, and resources of the AI Office and submit a report to the European Parliament and the Council [Art. 112(5)]. Later amendments to the Act are not reflected in this text.

Under the EU AI Act, a fundamental rights impact assessment (FRIA) is an assessment of the impact on fundamental rights that the use of a high-risk AI system may produce, which must be performed by certain deployers prior to deploying the system [Art. 27(1)].

**Who must perform it and when:**
* It is required prior to deploying a high-risk AI system referred to in Article 6(2) (except systems intended for use in the area listed in point 2 of Annex III) by deployers that are bodies governed by public law or private entities providing public services, as well as deployers of high-risk AI systems referred to in points 5(b) and (c) of Annex III [Art. 27(1)].
* The obligation applies to the first use of the high-risk AI system [Art. 27(2)]. Deployers may rely on previously conducted assessments or provider impact assessments in similar cases, but must update the assessment if any elements change or are no longer up to date [Art. 27(2)].

**Required content of the assessment:**
The assessment consists of:
* A description of the deployer's processes in which the system will be used in line with its intended purpose [Art. 27(1)(a)];
* A description of the time period and frequency with which the system is intended to be used [Art. 27(1)(b)];
* The categories of natural persons and groups likely to be affected in the specific context [Art. 27(1)(c)];
* The specific risks of harm likely to impact those categories or groups, taking into account information provided under Article 13 [Art. 27(1)(d)];
* A description of the implementation of human oversight measures according to the instructions for use [Art. 27(1)(e)];
* The measures to be taken if those risks materialise, including internal governance arrangements and complaint mechanisms [Art. 27(1)(f)].

**Relationship with DPIAs and administration:**
* If any of the FRIA obligations are already met through a data protection impact assessment (DPIA) under Regulation (EU) 2016/679 or Directive (EU) 2016/680, the FRIA complements that DPIA [Art. 27(4)].
* Deployers must notify the market surveillance authority of the results using a template questionnaire developed by the AI Office, unless exempted under Article 46(1) [Art. 27(3)], [Art. 27(5)].
* A summary of the findings must be submitted for the registration of the system in the EU database pursuant to Article 49(3) [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5].

Under the EU AI Act, record-keeping requirements for high-risk AI systems encompass technical logging capabilities and mandatory documentation retention:

**1. Automatic Logging Capabilities**
* **Lifetime logging:** High-risk AI systems must technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system [Art. 12(1)].
* **Traceability and purpose:** Logging capabilities must ensure traceability appropriate to the system's intended purpose by recording events relevant for:
  * identifying situations that may present a risk or lead to a substantial modification [Art. 12(2)(a)];
  * facilitating post-market monitoring [Art. 12(2)(b)];
  * monitoring system operation [Art. 12(2)(c)].
* **Specific minimum logging for systems under Annex III, point 1(a):** Systems falling under this category must, at a minimum, log:
  * the start and end date and time of each use [Art. 12(3)(a)];
  * the reference database checked against input data [Art. 12(3)(b)];
  * the input data that resulted in a match [Art. 12(3)(c)];
  * the identification of natural persons involved in verifying the results [Art. 12(3)(d)].
* **Retention of logs:** Providers must keep the automatically generated logs when such logs are under their control [Art. 16(e)].

**2. Quality Management System Procedures**
* Providers must include "systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information" as part of their written quality management system [Art. 17(1)(k)].

**3. Documentation Keeping by Providers**
* **10-year retention rule:** Providers must keep the following documents at the disposal of national competent authorities for 10 years after the system is placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)], [Art. 16(d)]:
  * technical documentation [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * quality management system documentation [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * documentation regarding changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * the EU declaration of conformity [Art. 18(1)(e)].
* **Financial institutions:** Providers subject to requirements under Union financial services law must maintain the technical documentation as part of the documentation kept under that relevant financial services law [Art. 18(3)].

The European Artificial Intelligence Board’s role is to advise and assist the Commission and the Member States to facilitate the consistent and effective application of the Regulation [Art. 66(e)], [Art. 66(f)]. 

To that end, the Board's role and tasks include:

* **Issuing recommendations and written opinions**, either at the request of the Commission or on its own initiative, on relevant matters such as [Art. 66(e)]:
  * The development and application of codes of conduct, codes of practice, and Commission guidelines [Art. 66(e)(i)];
  * The evaluation and review of the Regulation, serious incident reports, the EU database, delegated/implementing acts, and alignment with legislation in Annex I [Art. 66(e)(ii)];
  * Technical specifications, existing standards, harmonised standards, and common specifications [Art. 66(e)(iii)], [Art. 66(e)(iv)];
  * Trends in AI, including competitiveness, AI uptake, digital skills, and the evolving typology of AI value chains regarding accountability [Art. 66(e)(v)], [Art. 66(e)(vi)];
  * The potential need to amend Annex III or revise Article 5 [Art. 66(e)(vii)].
* **Promoting awareness**: Supporting the Commission in promoting AI literacy, public awareness, and understanding of the benefits, risks, safeguards, rights, and obligations relating to AI systems [Art. 66(f)].
* **Facilitating cooperation and coordination**: 
  * Establishing two standing sub-groups to provide platforms for cooperation and exchange among market surveillance authorities (acting as an ADCO under Regulation (EU) 2019/1020) and notifying authorities, as well as other sub-groups for specific issues [Art. 65(6)].
  * Enabling Member State representatives to facilitate consistency and coordination between national competent authorities regarding the implementation of the Regulation [Art. 65(4)(c)].

When applying with a notified body for the assessment of the technical documentation, the provider's application must include the following [Annex VII, point 4]:

* The name and address of the provider [Annex VII, point 4];
* A written declaration that the same application has not been lodged with any other notified body [Annex VII, point 4];
* The technical documentation referred to in Annex IV [Annex VII, point 4].

The technical documentation must contain, at a minimum, the elements set out in Annex IV [Art. 11(1)], [Annex IV]. However, SMEs (including start-ups) opting to provide the Annex IV elements in a simplified manner must use a simplified technical documentation form established by the Commission, which notified bodies are required to accept for the conformity assessment [Art. 11(1)].

Under the EU AI Act, high-risk AI systems must be accompanied by instructions for use provided in an appropriate digital format or otherwise, containing "concise, complete, correct and clear information that is relevant, accessible and comprehensible to deployers" [Art. 13(2)].

These instructions for use must contain at least the following information:

* **Provider details:** The identity and contact details of the provider and, where applicable, its authorised representative [Art. 13(3)(a)].
* **System characteristics, capabilities, and limitations of performance [Art. 13(3)(b)], including:**
  * Its intended purpose [Art. 13(3)(b)(i)];
  * The level of accuracy (including metrics), robustness, and cybersecurity against which it has been tested and validated and which can be expected, and any known and foreseeable circumstances impacting them [Art. 13(3)(b)(ii)], with declared accuracy levels and metrics specifically required to be declared in the instructions [Art. 15(3)];
  * Any known or foreseeable circumstances under intended use or reasonably foreseeable misuse that may lead to risks to health and safety or fundamental rights [Art. 13(3)(b)(iii)];
  * Where applicable, technical capabilities and characteristics to provide information relevant to explaining its output [Art. 13(3)(b)(iv)];
  * When appropriate, performance regarding specific persons or groups of persons [Art. 13(3)(b)(v)];
  * When appropriate, specifications for input data or relevant information on the training, validation, and testing datasets used [Art. 13(3)(b)(vi)];
  * Where applicable, information enabling deployers to interpret and appropriately use the output [Art. 13(3)(b)(vii)].
* **Pre-determined changes:** Any changes to the system and its performance pre-determined by the provider at the initial conformity assessment [Art. 13(3)(c)].
* **Human oversight:** Human oversight measures, including technical measures put in place to facilitate deployers' interpretation of system outputs [Art. 13(3)(d)].
* **Technical requirements and maintenance:** The computational and hardware resources needed, expected lifetime, and necessary maintenance and care measures (including frequency and software updates) to ensure proper functioning [Art. 13(3)(e)].
* **Logging:** Where relevant, a description of the mechanisms included within the system that allow deployers to properly collect, store, and interpret logs [Art. 13(3)(f)].
